we're nearly two weeks away from the  start of hybrid learning for  sixth ninth and twelfth graders in the  clark county school district  but the transition is annoying some  families and causing more questions than  answers  eight news now reporter kristen drummond  is live in the newsroom tonight with the  latest kristin  orko a few issues for families right now  they range from transportation  scheduling to not filling out a new  return  survey concerns as some families remain  uncertain  about their students going back in  buildings  there's just a lot of questions  around secondary frustration from  families  it's just irresponsible of the district  to not be  re-surveying parents universally rebecca  garcia criticizes ccsd  not offering a new hybrid survey for 4th  through 12th graders  the president of the nevada parent  teacher association tells me  some families want to switch their  choice from last year  more public health statistics have  changed the vaccine is now available  and all of us who filled it out in  november aren't getting a chance to  change our response  the decision partly stems from the  district trying to schedule  transportation for the kids who do  choose to come back they need at least a  three week  headway and if we did the whole survey  process again  from my understanding it would delay  kids coming back on campus at least  another week or two  tam lahnert is principal at spring  valley high school he  welcomes the return of students on  campus but knows the hybrid schedule  is not ideal they're only taking two  classes a day for two days a week it's  going to look very different  the hybrid plan shows secondary students  attend class in person twice a day  then leave campus to finish their other  courses remotely  the district allots 40 minutes between  dismissal and the next  class creating a logistics issue for  parents like alandra reynolds  we won't have time to be able to get him  back to make it back to class  a reason why garcia and others  questioned the district  not updating the plan there's just so  many challenges  with them some schools are re-surveying  families to know which option is best  for their students  either full-time distance education or  hybrid learning now the next  group going back are grades six nine and  twelve they're returning on march 22nd  all ccsd employees are going back into  the buildings  next monday reporting live in the  newsroom kristen drummond 8 news now
 

